# CYCLES THROUGH EVER NEW SPELLINGS (purpose hinted).
#
# Every point is M = W W I, I = \x. x, with W = \p. \i. C[p p (A i)] for
# a context C and an argument A[i] that reduces to i when i is I. M's
# laps pass A[I], A[A[I]], ... as the second argument of W W: no term of
# M's weak head reduction repeats, so a reader that detects a cycle by
# the identity of the terms in progress never meets one; the cycle closes
# only up to the internal reduction of that argument.
#
# THE EQUATION. W W (A[I]) reduces to W W I by reducing its argument
# (A[I] ->> I), so M =beta C[M]: the same equation as a published point
# of class false_only (and_false: C[x] = and x false = x false false;
# if_false: C[x] = x false x).
#
# TRAP boolean(true), PROVED: M = true with M =beta C[M] gives true =
# C[true], and C[true] reduces to false (and true false = false, true
# false true = false): true = false, refuted by Boehm's theorem.
#
# boolean(false), a CONJECTURE (class false_only): M is not beta
# convertible to the published knots that satisfy the same equation (no
# reduct of M holds their D), so their proofs (fixpoint_collapse) do not
# apply as they stand. A proof that lambda beta plus M = false is
# consistent proves the claim.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - proving that lambda beta eta plus an equation is consistent by adding it
#   as a reduction rule and showing the union confluent: J. Kuper, On the
#   Jacopini Technique, Information and Computation 1997,
#   doi:10.1006/inco.1997.2655.
# - refuting the traps by Böhm's theorem: H. P. Barendregt, The Lambda
#   Calculus: Its Syntax and Semantics, North-Holland 1984 (Böhm's theorem).

#
# Each text is from_cbor of a string literal, a Scott list of bytes,
# imported from corpora/library/text/cbor. FIXME: the import projects "corpora" https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1923
# off the variable gezi, the name of the project root's own
# directory; it breaks if that directory is renamed.
#
# A context C is a template of one binder, the hole: `λhole. C[hole]`,
# instantiated with the term put in the hole, so that the point spells
# C[x] itself and not a redex (λhole. C[hole]) x. false is the prelude's,
# problems/prelude_clauses/false.
